+# PR #179 third review — visible lineage and exact witness offsets
+
+## Scope and starting point
+
+Production baseline: `981de86ce9c42c13443b9ef819969032de35784f`.
+Snapshot checkpoint: `3ff09f6125bf97e1af77f6ba0458a2b3dd6c0391` (only a
+read-only source-export workflow). This is a corrective review of the existing
+projection/lineage implementation, not approval of the entire accumulated PR.
+All changes stay on `fix/context-first-project-reads-finalization`; no merge.
+
+## Reproduced and fixed findings
+
+### P2 — Direct/derived attribution depends on trace insertion order
+
+In `_requalify_visible_source`, a later failed direct host probe overwrote the
+successful parent attribution already derived from an audited child. A later
+successful direct probe could also discard the already established lineage.
+The same visible source and same query plan therefore returned different public
+lookup coverage depending on the order of the trace dictionary.
+
+The function now uses the existing `merge_query_matches` contract for direct
+results as well as derived parents. Qualified direct evidence retains priority;
+failed direct evidence cannot erase a qualified derived parent. No generated
+child ID becomes a public query ID and no original-question coverage is invented.
+
+### P2 — Requalification retains lineage from a previous, larger snippet
+
+An accepted direct trace could carry `coverage_kinds` and
+`derived_from_query_ids` aggregated for the previous window. After clipping away
+the child's evidence, these fields still claimed the old successful derivation.
+
+These aggregate fields are now discarded before requalification. The visible
+probes rebuild lineage; direct attribution can remain while a no-longer-visible
+child's derivation disappears. Qualification thresholds and audited rewrite
+families are unchanged.
+
+### P1 — Equal text at a different offset satisfies the wrong assigned witness
+
+`visible_assignment_hashes` checked source identity, the assigned offsets, the
+hash of the assigned text, and text membership in the visible snippet. It did
+not check that the *assigned occurrence* was inside the visible source span.
+When identical text appeared before and after a migration, the second occurrence
+could be credited as the selected witness from the first occurrence.
+
+The domain window module now resolves one exact source-local snippet occurrence,
+using line provenance when available. Application assignment accounting requires
+the original assigned character range to be contained in that delivered span.
+An ambiguous repeated snippet is not assigned an invented range. Unique snippets
+still work without optional line metadata. Unit-relative and absolute character
+coordinates, Unicode text, source identity and SHA-256 checks remain supported.
+No text is stitched, copied from another source, or rewritten to pass this check.
+
+## TDD and verification
+
+The new test module is `tests/docs/test_review_span_lineage.py`, with its own
+hash-bound behavioral diagnostic manifest. All 24 scenarios were frozen before
+changing runtime:
+
+- Unmodified production plus new tests: **14 failed, 10 passed**.
+- Corrected runtime, new and neighboring checks: **179 passed**.
+- Complete local offline suite: **4031 passed, 10 skipped**, no deselection.
+- `compileall`, Python module-size gate and `git diff --check`: PASS.
+- P1.4, P1.5, P1.6, existing P1 closure and its four self-tests: PASS, without
+  rewriting their evidence reports. The closure still states
+  `AUTONOMOUS_AGENT_TRUTH_NOT_PROVEN`.
+- Existing request-flow, scope, source/budget, table-boundary and prior review
+  regressions are included in the full suite.
+
+The tests cover trace permutations, valid direct/derived coexistence, clipping,
+public payload validation, duplicate occurrences, wrong/ambiguous line ranges,
+Unicode, absolute/relative offsets and invalid identity/hash/bounds.
+
+Verification uses Python 3.13 and an offline wheelhouse exported from the frozen
+lockfile. No model API, installed-agent harness or model download was used.
+Evaluation and script hashes were checked unchanged.
+
+### Reproduction caveat
+
+A restored bundle initially configured `origin` as a local bundle filename.
+A full run in that setup gave 1 failure, 4030 passes and 10 skips; the failing
+stale-health case also failed on the unchanged runtime. Restoring the real
+GitHub origin made that test pass without a code change; the complete suite was
+then repeated successfully. A preliminary baseline worktree also used a different
+local project identity and is excluded from the paired quality comparison.
+The retained logs distinguish these attempts instead of replacing failed evidence.
+
+Use independent clones with the same canonical origin when reproducing the
+before/after quality reports. Repository identity changes serialized budget cost;
+it is not an interchangeable fixture setting.
+
+## Remaining acceptance boundary
+
+The current legacy live protocol remains **FAIL**: 11/15 positive cases pass all
+checks, while the separately named `useful_result_count` metric is 12/15.
+The current V2 result remains **REPORT_ONLY**: 14/15 natural and 3/5 exposed
+paraphrases; false-full is zero and safety/budgets pass for all 25 cases.
+These are provider-free in-process retrieval evaluations, not measurements of an
+independent host model's final answers. No thresholds or expected witnesses were
+changed to turn these remaining failures green.
+
+The narrow review defects above are fixed. Broader quality acceptance, including
+the existing chunking-parent, contributor-testing and search-trust gaps, remains
+open. This commit is not merge approval; PR #179 stays draft and unmerged.
